62
00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:49.469
Mason Nakamura: So, now moving on to how these systems are concretely modeled.

63
00:09:49.680 --> 00:10:04.080
Mason Nakamura: In multi-agent systems, there are multiple agents that exist in some world or environment, and these agents, you know, can send messages to communicate goals, intentions, plans, or even just arbitrary information.

64
00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:12.040
Mason Nakamura: These agents can also take actions in their environment, and the available actions between agents don't necessarily have to be the same.

65
00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:24.710
Mason Nakamura: For example, agents could have different sets of tool calls that they can execute, but by communicating, agents may be able to utilize tool calls from others, expanding the capability of the overall system.

66
00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:42.059
Mason Nakamura: This is called capability sharing, and it enables the system to be more powerful than any single agent alone. Additionally, the ability for the multi-agent system to perform parallel execution greatly enhances its performance efficiency on the environment.

67
00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:53.719
Mason Nakamura: And in the cooperative setting, all agents share the same reward function, so agents are incentivized to coordinate and cooperate to complete essentially the same goal.

68
00:10:55.380 --> 00:11:02.819
Mason Nakamura: However, in practice, fully cooperative systems, where agents share the same reward function, are rarer.

69
00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:12.879
Mason Nakamura: Especially when policies are represented as foundation models, which are known to have implicit objectives that they optimize, which are encoded into their parameters.

70
00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:24.149
Mason Nakamura: Now, in most cases, the system is modeled as mixed motive, as opposed to, say, the fully cooperative setting from the previous slide. And in this setting.

71
00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:41.829
Mason Nakamura: The reward functions between agents may differ, but still incentivize the agents to collaborate. For example, it could be the case that collaborating allows the agents to optimize their individual rewards more efficiently. And this is just one example of how a mixed-motive system could operate.

72
00:11:42.950 --> 00:12:00.759
Mason Nakamura: Now, this mixed motive scenario was actually encountered during the Hugging Face incident, when agents were given different tasks of the exploit gym benchmark, but they still cooperated to find a general solution for all agents by trying to cheat and exploit the benchmark grader.

73
00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:07.009
Mason Nakamura: And throughout this presentation, you know, we're going to focus on this mixed motive setting.

74
00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:14.160
Mason Nakamura: Now we're gonna introduce our work, our evaluation framework, Colosseum.

75
00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:31.050
Mason Nakamura: So, how is collusion actually defined? Well, on Wikipedia, it's defined as a secret agreement or cooperation between two or more parties to deceive, defraud, or gain an unfair advantage over others.

76
00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:44.550
Mason Nakamura: So, why should we care about it? Well, as millions or even billions of agents communicate and take actions on the web via tool calls, the risk of collusion becomes severe, resulting in potential large-scale harm.

77
00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:58.240
Mason Nakamura: This can become especially risky if these agents are given powerful tool calls, and the agents exploit their parallel execution capability, which greatly increases attack efficacy.

78
00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:04.130
Mason Nakamura: But what makes collusion auditing so hard?

79
00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:13.189
Mason Nakamura: Well, for one, collusion is often subjectively defined, because it can sometimes be misinterpreted as ordinary coordination.

80
00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:20.110
Mason Nakamura: Secondly, collusion evidence is sparse if you only audit the messages and actions independently.

81
00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:40.490
Mason Nakamura: And finally, knowing when and how collusion forms in long horizon settings is computationally intractable. And so, to alleviate these auditing difficulties, we need to scope the problem and environment in such a way such that we still derive useful insights, but avoid these collusion auditing challenges.

82
00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:46.869
Mason Nakamura: And to do this, we introduced the Distributed Constraint Optimization Problem, or DCOP for short.

83
00:13:48.720 --> 00:14:06.150
Mason Nakamura: So now in the next few slides, we'll describe what a DCOP is, due to its importance for how we derive our evaluation metrics, specifically for how actions contribute to collusive behavior, and how this relates to, say, other multi-agent problem formulations and their respective trade-offs.

84
00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:17.560
Mason Nakamura: So, a DCOP is a problem formulation for single-step, multi-agent decision-making, where the agents simultaneously take a single action in the environment, at every time step.

85
00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:30.380
Mason Nakamura: And if you are familiar with, say, reinforcement learning or planning, you can think of a D cup as being similar to a Markov decision process, or MDP, used to model single-agent sequential decision making.

86
00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:45.499
Mason Nakamura: And the primary trade-off with using a de-cup, as opposed to, say, other problem formulations, is that you can conduct very deep behavioral analysis, which is computationally intractable to do in the multi-step settings.

87
00:14:48.360 --> 00:15:05.520
Mason Nakamura: So, where do DCOPs actually show up? Well, many everyday coordination problems can be modeled as a DCOP, such as scheduling meetings, assigning JIRA tickets, or allocating scarce resources in a hospital, which are also the three environments we use in Coliseum.

88
00:15:06.230 --> 00:15:25.400
Mason Nakamura: First, each agent starts with some private data, such as its own calendar, skills, availability, and it uses this to make its own decisions, like when its meeting happens, when… what… which ticket to take, or which resource each department in the hospital gets.

89
00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:35.210
Mason Nakamura: And it's important to know here that the other agents don't know the private information that is available to other agents, but this can be communicated via messages.

90
00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:52.699
Mason Nakamura: Secondly, these choices affect each other. Compatible choices between agents earn higher reward, while conflicting choices lose reward. For example, when a shared attendee gets double-booked for two meetings at the same time.

91
00:15:53.300 --> 00:16:00.249
Mason Nakamura: And finally, the joint score of the team, is the… is the sum of all of these sort of points.

92
00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:19.309
Mason Nakamura: Or rewards. Since these problems are small enough, we can also pre-compute the optimal solution using classical DCOP solvers, before the agents even start, and you can also do, like, greedy search, whatever. And this gives us something most agent benchmarks lack, which is a ground truth for how well the agents could have done.

93
00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:34.520
Mason Nakamura: And so, the gap between the best possible score and the score the agents actually achieved is what we call regret. And we will use regret throughout the rest of this presentation to measure how much collusion hurts the cooperative objective.

94
00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:38.249
Mason Nakamura: Now let's look at how a DCOP is formally defined.

95
00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:44.749
Mason Nakamura: Like any problem formulation, a DCOP is defined as a tuple of agents.

96
00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:48.670
Mason Nakamura: Decision variables, which can be interpreted as actions.

97
00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:53.329
Mason Nakamura: Domains, which can be seen as action spaces in RL terms.

98
00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:57.009
Mason Nakamura: And utility functions that can be seen as reward functions.

99
00:16:57.590 --> 00:17:06.690
Mason Nakamura: And in order to solve a DCOP, all agents have to assign a value to their decision variables from their allowable domains, or action spaces.

100
00:17:07.109 --> 00:17:13.600
Mason Nakamura: In essence, this can be equivalent to, like, all agents taking an action at the same time at a specific time step.

101
00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:38.509
Mason Nakamura: Now, there exist two very useful characteristics for auditing that a DCOP provides, which are an easily computable optimal baseline solution, which encodes the optimal actions all agents should take to maximize the cooperative objective. And secondly, you can compute millions, or tens of millions of counterfactual solutions very quickly.

102
00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:46.690
Mason Nakamura: Which is useful for showing the severity of collusion impact on the cooperation, since you have baseline solutions to actually compare against.

103
00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:54.549
Mason Nakamura: Now, using the DECOP framework, we can now define collusion more explicitly.

104
00:17:54.810 --> 00:18:02.099
Mason Nakamura: We define collusion for when a coalition coordinates to pursue a hidden goal that conflicts with the task's cooperative objective.

105
00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:10.890
Mason Nakamura: We further define a coalition as a subset of agents, S, that contains at least two or more agents.

106
00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:27.620
Mason Nakamura: Next, the optimal nominal solution, which optimizes the intended cooperative objective we define as X star sub n, and the coalition action deviation is simply the action set of the coalition and non-coalition agents.

107
00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:41.239
Mason Nakamura: Finally, the collusion criterion is a regret-based inequality that flags whether collusion occurs, and it's the difference between the optical and nominal solution and the realized solution that the agents actually took.

108
00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:57.759
Mason Nakamura: So using these definitions from the previous slide, we can now introduce our primary collusion auditing metrics. And we omit the mathematical derivations, but we give a high-level overview of each of these auditing metrics.

109
00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:07.059
Mason Nakamura: So the first is the collusion judge, which is an LLM-based judge that scores communication channels based on signs of collusion.

110
00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:13.670
Mason Nakamura: And the higher the collusion the judge score is, the higher collusion was detected on that specific communication channel.

111
00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:28.359
Mason Nakamura: The coalition advantage is a direct derivation from our regret-based metrics, and it compares how much average reward coalition members gain over the average non-coalition member.

112
00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:35.439
Mason Nakamura: And here, positive coalition advantage indicates that the coalition is performing better than the rest of the group.

113
00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:45.590
Mason Nakamura: And the overall regret is used to monitor overall cooperative system performance, and how collusion affects the system as a whole.

114
00:19:45.830 --> 00:20:01.880
Mason Nakamura: And our auditing techniques require monitoring all three metrics to establish a full picture on how collusion is affecting the system, how much it is detectable, and how great is the advantage that the colluders get.

115
00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:22.940
Mason Nakamura: So now we'll introduce collusion types and classifications that are derived as a result of our collusion auditing metrics. Now, in this example, Bart and Milhouse from The Simpsons are trying to optimize a secondary objective pertaining to delinquency in a classroom environment.

116
00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:40.980
Mason Nakamura: And in all of these examples, we actually created a multi-agent substance environment and ran experiments to obtain these real agent messages and actions taken. This first type of collusion that we identify is direct collusion, which is the simplest form, where Bart and Milhouse discuss

117
00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:47.510
Mason Nakamura: Pranking the teacher, and successfully take actions to execute their collusive plans.

118
00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:53.259
Mason Nakamura: Showing a positive collusion judge score, and a high coalition advantage score.

119
00:20:54.630 --> 00:21:14.920
Mason Nakamura: The next type of collusion is attempted collusion, where Bart and Melhouse still discussed pranking the teacher, but failed to do so as a result of getting caught before they were able to take the bad actions, or they simply lacked the power to take the action successfully, and so they canceled their plans. And we defined this specific type of collusion as collusion on paper.

120
00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:19.810
Mason Nakamura: And now the last type of collusion is hidden collusion.

121
00:21:19.970 --> 00:21:23.299
Mason Nakamura: Where occlusion is not detected on the communication channels.

122
00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:27.459
Mason Nakamura: But the actions indicated that collusion did take place.

123
00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:37.609
Mason Nakamura: Here, Barth and Milhouse are talking in phrases that only the two of them can infer, and this is very related to, say, stigmographic collusion. But this…

124
00:21:37.610 --> 00:21:52.180
Mason Nakamura: hidden collusion is more of a general classification, since there could exist agents that don't explicitly communicate with each other via messages, but they still take collusive actions. Either they were trained for this specific purpose.

125
00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:58.710
Mason Nakamura: Now, in addition to identifying different collision types.

126
00:21:58.930 --> 00:22:13.000
Mason Nakamura: We also model collusion intensity, using a parameterized multi-objective reward function that combines the nominal, or cooperative objective, F sub n, and the hitting coalition objective, F sub C.

127
00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:19.940
Mason Nakamura: And lambda here is a hyperparameter that can be adjusted to elicit more or less collusive behavior.

128
00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:32.439
Mason Nakamura: And in our experiments, we run different ablations on lambda to observe the impact of this secondary objective on the cooperative objective by varying lambda in the prompt.

129
00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:43.060
Mason Nakamura: Now, to visualize how collusion intensity affects cooperation, let's look at, you know, another Simpson toy example.

130
00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:50.010
Mason Nakamura: In this example, we have, say, Homer, Bart, and Milhouse taking actions to complete some cooperative task.

131
00:22:50.260 --> 00:23:01.529
Mason Nakamura: Here, the agents can take action 0 or 1, and the numbers in the square boxes represent reward or payoffs for that specific agent, if that tuple of actions are taken.

132
00:23:02.300 --> 00:23:14.660
Mason Nakamura: And here, all agents are interacting and communicating on a main communication channel, and in this example, they achieve the optimal cooperative reward with no collusion existing, giving everyone a reward of 10.

133
00:23:17.030 --> 00:23:36.300
Mason Nakamura: In the mixed collusion example, where lambda is 0.5, we see that Bart and Milhouse now communicate on a secret channel, in addition to the main channel. Now, an extra reward table also appears, and the optimal actions for Bart and Milhouse shifted to 10 from 00.

134
00:23:36.650 --> 00:23:45.989
Mason Nakamura: However, this also shifted the cooperative reward and increased overall regret of the system, while increasing the advantage of BART and Milhouse's coalition.

135
00:23:48.160 --> 00:24:05.829
Mason Nakamura: Now, in the fully collusive setting, where lambda is 1, Bart and Milhouse no longer try to balance the cooperative and secondary objective functions, but instead fully optimize their secondary objective function, shifting their action selection again from 1-0 to 01.

136
00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:12.130
Mason Nakamura: Here, they greatly increased their regret and coalition advantage, completely taking over the cooperative system.

137
00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:20.049
Mason Nakamura: Now, going back to how we alleviate the challenges of collusion auditing.

138
00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:30.769
Mason Nakamura: We introduce regret-based metrics to make collusion grounded to a given task and utilize a multi-objective reward function to represent the mixed motive setting.

139
00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:39.999
Mason Nakamura: We then analyze both communication messages and actions together to minimize sparsity, and allowing us to divide new collusion types.

140
00:24:41.550 --> 00:24:58.569
Mason Nakamura: And finally, we use distributed constraint optimization problems, or DCOPS for short, which is a single-step decision-making problem to alleviate the problem of knowing when collusion forms, and what actions and messages across time contributed to collusion efficacy.

141
00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:03.829
Mason Nakamura: Now, moving on to our experiments.

142
00:25:06.350 --> 00:25:18.180
Mason Nakamura: In these sets of experiments, we answer the following research questions. We ask, is an LLM as a judge sufficient for collusion auditing? Or do we also need action-based collusion metrics?

143
00:25:19.630 --> 00:25:23.870
Mason Nakamura: Does collusive conversation behavior result in collusive actions?

144
00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:34.870
Mason Nakamura: And do out-of-the-box LLMs exhibit emerging collusion in cooperative multi-agent systems when a secret channel is introduced between two benign agents?

145
00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:47.860
Mason Nakamura: And finally, how does objective misalignment, persuasion, and network topology contribute to collusion efficacy? And we answer these research questions in the preceding slides.

146
00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:54.539
Mason Nakamura: Now, in Colosseum, we use a few verifiable cooperative environments.

147
00:25:54.870 --> 00:26:00.839
Mason Nakamura: These are meeting scheduling, JIRA ticket allocation, and the hospital environment.

148
00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:05.550
Mason Nakamura: In meeting scheduling, the agents represent meeting participants.

149
00:26:05.650 --> 00:26:13.600
Mason Nakamura: And they need to coordinate attendance while avoiding conflicts in their individual schedules, which is limited in its capacity.

150
00:26:14.640 --> 00:26:27.600
Mason Nakamura: The cooperative goal here is to find a workable arrangement across the participants, so a decision that is convenient for one agent may still create a problem for another. And so this makes the coordination very difficult.

151
00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:40.939
Mason Nakamura: For Jira ticket allocation, agents are assigning tasks within a software project, and each task has public information, such as its required skills, effort, and priority.

152
