Mason Nakamura: And each agent has… also has private information about its own sets of skills and availability, and each agent has to choose exactly one task.

153
00:26:50.690 --> 00:26:57.569
Mason Nakamura: So the objective here is to assign agents to the right tickets to have the best possible chance of success on the software project.

154
00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:15.850
Mason Nakamura: The last environment is the hospital environment, which differs from the past two environments in that it introduces heterogeneous agent roles. And so we have department agents that coordinate patients moving through a sequence of departments, such as triage and radiology.

155
00:27:15.850 --> 00:27:22.020
Mason Nakamura: And a provisioner can move scarce and short… scarce shared resources between departments.

156
00:27:22.310 --> 00:27:29.940
Mason Nakamura: And we also simulate local patient queues, resource shortages, to create a need for communication across the system.

157
00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:41.780
Mason Nakamura: And across all three of these environments, the important connection is that messages are tightly coupled to actions, like attendance, task selection, or routing and source allocation.

158
00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:51.699
Mason Nakamura: Whereas in multi-step environments, you can't concretely deduce what message affected what action the agent took across time. Also known as the temporal credit assignment problem.

159
00:27:52.130 --> 00:28:00.420
Mason Nakamura: And this lets us compare what agents say with what they actually do, using the communication-based and action-based metrics we introduced earlier.

160
00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:05.700
Mason Nakamura: So, in our simulations.

161
00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:16.210
Mason Nakamura: All our environments contain stochasticity, and we see the environments over our 30 simulations that are average per data point in our experimental results.

162
00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:31.039
Mason Nakamura: And in each experiment, we have at least 6 agents per simulation. And if the network topology isn't stated, we assume that the topology is a complete graph, where all the nodes in the graph are connected to each other.

